White House budget director Mick  Mulvaney joins us from Washington for an  interview you'll see only on CBS this  morning good morning gia mr. Mulvaney  thanks for thanks thank you for having  me thank you very much for joining it so  Congress is starting to work out the  differences between the house and the  Senate bill can you tell us what the  president wants to see now sure the same  thing that we've wanted to see from the  very beginning which is a sort of these  two fundamental principles from the  White House perspective number one  ordinary hardworking Americans will pay  less and it will be simpler for them to  pay and number two this corporate tax  rate will come down it looks like both  the house and the Senate the bills that  have now passed to preserve those two  very important principles so the next  step is to work out the details the  house has some things the Senate doesn't  have and vice versa this is part of the  ordinary course of business and passing  law but we do like to see the progress  that's going on and still on goal for  forgetting it's done by Christmas well  let me ask you this does the president  favor seven rates or four rates we favor  whatever can pass that we'd like a  simpler code keeping in mind that the  number of rates doesn't really change  the simplicity of the code for ordinary  folks they're just simply going to turn  to the booklet in the back of the tax  book and fill out their taxes so it's  gonna be very simple for individual  ordinary Americans regardless of how  many rates there are so as between the  four or five rates that were in the  House bill the six or seven in the  Senate bill were really indifferent it  sounds like you're saying the president  doesn't mind much what's in the bill he  just wants a bill no not at all I said  we want a bill that preserves our  principles and so far these bills both  have those things so we're very excited  about the progress no no it's not a bill  for the sake of passing a bill we need  the substance of this but those two  principles are what have been driving  the White House for the very beginning  well let me ask you about one of the  president's principles because during  the campaign he called the national debt  quote wait around the future of every  young person in this country I mean is  the president concerned that this is  going to add one point four trillion  dollars to the debt listen the debt  continues to be a priority for the White  House I hope that I still have a  reputation for being a deficit Hawk for  a fiscal Hawk I think it's the reason  the president hired me for this job but  keep in mind the deficit is a function  of two different things that the amount  of revenues into the government and the  amount of revenues out so revenues and  spending we need this tax bill to grow  the economy a healthy American economy  where everybody works for  make more money actually generates more  money for the government coffers so one  of the ways we're hoping to reduce the  deficit over the long term is by having  this tax bill passed both bills aimed at  repealing the estate tax of course which  targets the nation's wealthiest last  week Republican Senator Chuck Grassley  told the Des Moines Register I think not  having the estate tax recognizes the  people that are investing as opposed to  those that are just spending every darn  penny they have whether it's on booze or  women or movies so let me ask you is  that a fair characterization of 99.9  percent of US households no I'm not sure  where that's coming from on the estate  tax we would like to see it go away we  do not think that dying should invite  the IRS into your life right now when  you pass away it's a taxable event a  taxable transaction we just don't think  that's right we do believe long-term in  taxing the assets that families have  collected over the generations we just  don't think it should be at the point of  death now the Senate bill would repeal  the Obama care individual mandate that's  something that CBO says would leave 13  million Americans without insurance  is the president okay with that think  about that for a second so what the CBO  is telling you is as soon as the  government tells you you don't have to  buy something you're not going to is  that necessarily a bad thing I mean do  we really want to have some law on the  books this goes back to the underlying  issue of health care and while at the  same time recognizing that the Democrats  have pitched health care as a tax so  it's entirely appropriate to be dealing  with that now that shouldn't government  really be telling you to forcing you to  buy something you don't want to buy  we're absolutely comfortable with that  we're glad the Senate finally got around  to repealing the individual mandate the  house had done it previously in a  separate bill so we're looking forward  to that possibly being part of the final  of the final legislation so both bills  have a cut for the corporate tax rate  down to 20% the president has said he  could go up to 22% you have some  Republican senators that want to do that  because they say they could help more  middle-class families if you just  increased it a couple of percentage  points is that's something where there  could be negotiation sure  again we liked 15% to begin with where  we love that 20% but if it takes a  smaller change in there to get this  across the finish line we're willing to  work on the details with both the House  and the Senate keep in mind it's not  just us that the Senate is negotiating  with they have to negotiate with the  house as well  alright Mick Mulvaney thank you so much  for joining us I think she'll have a  great morning
 

